\section{The anchor depends on matched depth}{

\code{"reference"} subtracts the mean of the Ig-.1 vs Ig-.2 pair, which is ~0 only while
the fractions are at a common depth. It is not a general-purpose estimate of the
anchor: with \code{rarefy_by_sample = FALSE} in \code{\link[=group_sorted_samples]{group_sorted_samples()}} it subtracts
\code{log2(S_neg1/S_neg2)} from a quantity carrying \code{log2(S_pos/S_neg1)}, two different
constants. On \code{ps_igseq} that translated whole samples by -1.77 to +1.20 Z units and
\strong{inverted the conclusion}: sample_4 read 5 enriched / 29 depleted rarefied against
44 / 5 unrarefied, sample_5 1 / 10 against 13 / 0. Keep \code{rarefy_by_sample = TRUE}
unless you have corrected the depth ratio yourself; the anchor argument above assumes
it throughout.

Taxa flagged via \code{was_imputed} form a \strong{separate population}, scored against a
single pooled mean/sd taken over all of them at once rather than mixed into the
sliding windows. Two separate reasons, both measured on \code{ps_igseq} under
\code{pseudo_count}: their null is 2.8x-6.9x wider than that of taxa with no imputed
zero and they barely overlap the rest in abundance, so windowing them \emph{with} the
rest would inflate their Z-scores several-fold; and their abundance axis is largely
an artefact of the imputation -- 75-83\% of them share an \code{obs_abundance} with
another taxon -- so windowing them \emph{among themselves} estimates the scale from tied,
arbitrary slices and deflates it by up to 5x, which inflates the Z-scores just as
badly in the other direction. Pooling is what neither failure mode reaches.

Independently of that split, \code{estimate_from} marks rows that are scored but may not
\emph{inform} a window's mean/sd. This is how out-of-cone taxa on the
\code{"purity_corrected"} change axis are handled: their reference value is saturated
rather than measured, so it must not enter the estimate, but they still receive a
score on their window's scale. Unlike imputed taxa they are not segregated, because
measurement shows they sit at the same abundances as the rest and their null spread
is within noise of it.

That cone exclusion is deliberately \strong{not} applied inside the imputed population,
where the flag stops reporting measurement quality and starts reporting the
imputation pattern. A taxon zeroed in only one of the two negative fractions gets an
extreme ratio and falls outside the cone; one zeroed in neither stays inside. Masking
on the cone there therefore keeps only the taxa whose negative fractions were both
measured and discards every censored pair -- and censored pairs are the dominant mode
of genuine replicate noise in this assay, not an edge case (see
\code{\link[=get_ma_coordinates]{get_ma_coordinates()}}'s Uninformative pairs section). Measured on \code{ps_igseq} under
\code{pseudo_count}, masking halves the reference sd (pooled 2.2-3.6 versus masked
1.0-1.8, ratio 0.47-0.64), which would roughly double every imputed taxon's \verb{|Z|}.

The pairs that genuinely carry no information -- \emph{both} fractions zero, so the change
is one substituted value over itself -- are cut upstream instead, by
\code{\link[=get_ma_coordinates]{get_ma_coordinates()}}, which marks them \code{NA} on the affected axis; \code{.slide_z_params()}
then drops them from the estimate, and from the numerator too when they are the
observed pair. That is a cleaner cut than the cone, because it targets exactly the
fabricated values without touching the censored ones. Before it existed those pairs
were reported as a change of exactly 0: on \code{ps_igseq} under \code{pseudo_count} they made
up 13-43\% of this population's reference values and deflated its pooled sd by 7-31\%,
and every taxon whose Ig+ and Ig-.1 were both zero received the same single
manufactured Z-score as all the others.

Even so, neither remaining subset of this population is clean -- the censored ones
depend on the pseudocount for their magnitude, the out-of-cone ones are saturated --
so pooling over all of them is the least-wrong estimate, not a good one. Their \verb{|Z|}
remains a floor rather than a test, exactly as for the saturated taxa described in
\code{\link[=get_slide_z]{get_slide_z()}}.
}

